WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.380
A touching tribute to the late&nbsp;
Chadwick Boseman that's also a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:07.020
thrilling new installment in&nbsp;
the MCU saga? "Black Panther:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:11.700
Wakanda Forever" was a tall order, but&nbsp;
Marvel Studios pulled it off. Here's how.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:17.160
"Wakanda Forever" is the 30th feature film&nbsp;
in the Marvel Cinematic Universe. By now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:22.260
audiences are so familiar with the MCU's opening&nbsp;
credits format, we could all probably reproduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:26.520
the moving letters and rousing music from&nbsp;
memory. But Marvel has been experimenting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.240
with its recognizable logo as of late.&nbsp;
The titles for "Thor: Love and Thunder"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.160
swapped the traditional MCU soundtrack for&nbsp;
a Guns &amp; Roses-esque power ballad version,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:39.600
and "Werewolf by Night" bypassed them&nbsp;
altogether for its own short, snappy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:44.580
and brightly colored introduction. But Marvel&nbsp;
Studios' opening credits have never looked or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:50.340
sounded like they do in "Wakanda Forever," and&nbsp;
they've never been used for such a purpose before.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:55.500
The standard sequence sees hand-drawn Marvel&nbsp;
Comics illustrations morph into live-action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:00.900
Marvel Studios film clips, all on the surface&nbsp;
of 3D block letters. As each new superhero is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:05.760
introduced, they get added to the next entry's&nbsp;
graphics. But this time, rather than the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:11.700
stable of Marvel superheroes being represented,&nbsp;
every still and clip is of Chadwick Boseman as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:17.820
T'Challa. For the first time, there's no music&nbsp;
underscoring the visuals — it's 30 seconds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:22.920
respectful silence. As the words "Marvel Studios"&nbsp;
fix themselves in the center of the screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:27.540
they briefly glow purple -- the shade of the&nbsp;
heart-shaped herb -- before fading into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:32.700
silver and black of T'Challa's Black Panther suit.&nbsp;
There's nothing especially deep about superficial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:37.800
changes to a corporate logo, but they help to&nbsp;
set the tone for the film that's about to unfold.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:42.540
Boseman's tragic death in August&nbsp;
of 2020 meant that "Black Panther:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:47.460
Wakanda Forever" had to be re-written from a&nbsp;
script that was already in the works. Fans and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:52.140
filmmakers alike debated about the best way&nbsp;
forward for the franchise. Given the choice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:57.360
between recasting the main character or elevating&nbsp;
the status of one of the supporting players,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:02.160
director and co-writer Ryan Coogler opted for&nbsp;
the latter and got to work re-tooling the script.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:07.920
The movie opens with a heart-wrenching scene in&nbsp;
which Shuri is racing against the clock to improve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:13.020
the formula of her synthetic heart-shaped herb.&nbsp;
But before she can test out the unlikely cure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:17.820
her mother, Queen Ramonda, announces that&nbsp;
T'Challa has gone to his ancestors. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:22.260
Black Panther's death from an illness that&nbsp;
modern medicine — even Wakanda's — can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:27.600
treat is an obvious allusion to Boseman's&nbsp;
own death from colon cancer at the age of 43.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.940
It was both smart screenwriting and compassionate&nbsp;
collaboration that led to this decision. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:37.140
Coogler explained to The Hollywood Reporter,&nbsp;
Boseman's family consulted on the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:42.900
which ensured that the final product would show&nbsp;
the late actor proper deference. Other franchises&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:47.700
have had to deal with similar issues, though not&nbsp;
to this extent. For example, Carrie Fisher's death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:52.080
between production on "The Last Jedi" and "The&nbsp;
Rise of Skywalker" deeply affected the final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.580
chapter of the "Star Wars" saga. She was expected&nbsp;
to play a larger role in the third movie in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:02.100
sequel trilogy, but her death changed those plans.&nbsp;
By not straying too far from the truth, "Wakanda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:07.680
Forever" allows us to mourn Boseman and T'Challa&nbsp;
at the same time without trivializing the tragedy.

00:03:08.806 --> 00:03:13.560
The primary way we and the characters&nbsp;
in "Wakanda Forever" mourn the loss of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.760
the real actor and the fictional King is&nbsp;
through T'Challa's emotional and elaborate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:22.200
funeral festivities. In "Captain America:&nbsp;
Civil War," T'Challa talks to Natasha about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:25.680
how he's processing his own father's&nbsp;
death after the bombing in Vienna.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:30.519
"In my culture, death is not the end."

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:36.120
Since Wakandans believe in an afterlife&nbsp;
and a spiritual realm, the sad occasion of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:40.320
the King's funeral is both an opportunity&nbsp;
to grieve and a celebration of his life.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:45.120
Pulling from African cultural and religious&nbsp;
heritage, Wakanda's people gather in gauzy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:49.800
white ceremonial clothing. There's&nbsp;
dancing, singing and mourning. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:53.640
are plenty of nods to Chadwick Boseman's&nbsp;
vaunted place in the MCU as well.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:58.860
During the funeral, we see a shot of two&nbsp;
drummers playing in front of a mural of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:02.520
King T'Challa. Wakandan letters surround&nbsp;
a black and white painting of the fallen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:05.760
Black Panther's face. Production&nbsp;
designer Hannah Beachler actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:08.580
developed a written language&nbsp;
for Wakanda in the first film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:13.680
according to IndieWire. From what we can see, some&nbsp;
of those characters are used here to spell out,

00:04:16.851 --> 00:04:21.060
The mural gives filmmakers a way to show&nbsp;
T'Challa's face without Boseman's physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:25.560
presence and without resorting to flashbacks&nbsp;
too often. But it's also a reference to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:29.640
something that really happened in the wake&nbsp;
of the beloved actor's death. Disneyland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:33.900
commissioned an official mural of Chadwick&nbsp;
Boseman that was displayed at Downtown Disney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:39.000
and it eventually moved to a children's hospital,&nbsp;
where it will remain as a permanent installation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:43.020
Other murals began to pop up around the&nbsp;
country to honor the man who had been a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:47.700
hero and an inspiration to so many. The mural&nbsp;
in "Wakanda Forever" is an acknowledgement of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:52.925
T'Challa's importance to the Wakandans and of&nbsp;
Boseman's importance to multiple communities.

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:57.780
The Wakandan alphabet can be spotted&nbsp;
throughout both "Black Panther" films. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:02.520
"Wakanda Forever," Riri Williams is taken back&nbsp;
to the secretive African nation and sees their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:07.260
rich culture and advanced technology for the&nbsp;
first time. As she's given a sort of VIP tour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:11.460
the Dora Milaje stand guard in front&nbsp;
of rectangular columns which are lit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:16.440
from within and decorated with Wakandan&nbsp;
characters. Each one contains a word or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:20.772
phrase, and the five that are most&nbsp;
visible during Riri's visit spell,

00:05:25.231 --> 00:05:29.460
It appears the designers added this&nbsp;
detail as an Easter egg and an extra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:31.800
way to stamp Boseman's memory onto the set.

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:37.260
That nobody can replace T'Challa as king or&nbsp;
as Black Panther in the movie becomes a meta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:42.420
commentary on the idea that nobody could really&nbsp;
replace Chadwick Boseman in the role. Wakanda is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:48.420
without its protector because Erik Killmonger, aka&nbsp;
N'Jadaka, burned the reserves of the heart-shaped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:52.193
herb so that no one could challenge him&nbsp;
during the events of the original film.

00:05:52.481 --> 00:05:53.720
"Burn it all!"

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:59.760
So, as "Wakanda Forever" begins, literally no&nbsp;
one can replace T'Challa since the enhanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:04.080
powers of the Black Panther aren't available&nbsp;
to them without their vibranium-laced plants.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:09.360
Queen Ramonda is acting as Regent, Shuri seems&nbsp;
more interested in science than politics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:14.400
and M'Baku is content to lead the Jabari&nbsp;
and be an ally of the Wakandan crown. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:17.820
since Shuri has given up on the idea of&nbsp;
creating a synthetic heart-shaped herb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:22.560
everybody else has given up on the idea of&nbsp;
there ever being a Black Panther again. Indeed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:28.140
there isn't one until the climax of "Wakanda&nbsp;
Forever." Narratively, this works well to give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:33.240
Boseman his due and to build anticipation for&nbsp;
the moment when Shuri finally takes the mantle.

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:38.400
One of the reasons Chadwick Boseman's&nbsp;
loss hit so hard among moviegoers who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:42.600
didn't know him personally was because&nbsp;
the actor gave off such a warm, authentic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:46.680
and eminently likable energy. He&nbsp;
brought that energy to T'Challa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:51.480
who was among the more stoic characters&nbsp;
in the MCU. Black Panther didn't get quips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:57.180
and gags like Iron Man, Ant-Man, Star Lord, or&nbsp;
Doctor Strange. He was the MCU's straight man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:03.360
even more so than Captain America. In stark&nbsp;
contrast, Killmonger — his cousin and antagonist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:08.220
in "Black Panther" — got to chew the scenery&nbsp;
while T'Challa remained regal and restrained.

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:13.860
Michael B. Jordan gets to come back in one of&nbsp;
the MCU's more purposeful cameos as Killmonger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:18.240
who didn't make it out of the last movie&nbsp;
alive. When Shuri enters the ancestral plane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:22.860
she wants to see her brother or her&nbsp;
mother. Instead, she sees Killmonger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:26.100
who shares the rage she has&nbsp;
at having lost her family.

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:31.800
It would've been unrealistic for Killmonger&nbsp;
— a notoriously confrontational and viciously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:37.200
honest character — to have softened too much&nbsp;
toward his former rival. What N'Jadaka has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:41.880
say to Shuri about her brother is completely&nbsp;
in keeping with his character. He admits that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:46.500
T'Challa was a good man, but warns Shuri&nbsp;
that he was too noble for his own good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:52.200
Shuri takes this advice to heart, waging&nbsp;
war against Talocan until she realizes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:57.322
her and Namor's thirst for vengeance can only&nbsp;
end in calamity for both of their nations.

00:07:58.190 --> 00:08:03.420
After the climactic battle in "Wakanda Forever,"&nbsp;
Shuri heads to Haiti to burn her funeral clothes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:09.060
and finally grieve her brother's passing in&nbsp;
peace. Shots of the fibers burning are intercut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.060 --> 00:08:13.980
with breathtaking shots of Shuri in profile&nbsp;
beginning to cry. When she closes her eyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:18.960
we see a montage of her memories of T'Challa,&nbsp;
made up of Boseman's highlights from his four-film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:24.540
tenure in the MCU. These clips crossfade into&nbsp;
the end credits, accompanied by Rihanna's new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:28.676
original song, "Lift Me Up," the lyrics&nbsp;
of which include the repeated phrase,

00:08:31.591 --> 00:08:36.840
It's undoubtedly the quietest, calmest,&nbsp;
and most earnest ending in MCU history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:41.640
This symbolic end to Shuri's mourning period&nbsp;
is also incredibly cathartic for the audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:46.740
who's probably dabbing the tears from their&nbsp;
eyes when the final title card appears, reading,

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:54.960
"It's a beautiful contribution&nbsp;
to Chadwick's legacy."

00:08:55.560 --> 00:09:01.380
Usually, Marvel's mid and end-credits stingers&nbsp;
are reserved for teasing new projects and making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:07.380
tongue-in-cheek jokes. "Wakanda Forever" uses its&nbsp;
lone and longer-than-usual mid-credits scene to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:12.840
touchingly and gracefully transition the "Black&nbsp;
Panther" franchise-within-a-franchise onto the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:17.700
next leg of its journey. While Shuri's still&nbsp;
having a moment on the beach, Nakia walks down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:22.500
to where she's sitting with a young boy. She&nbsp;
introduces him as her son and Shuri's nephew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:27.480
Toussaint. As it happens, T'Challa prepared&nbsp;
Nakia and their secret son for his death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:32.820
and the family of two has been staying away from&nbsp;
whatever drama will await the young Prince when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:38.220
comes of age. Shuri is heartened to find out that&nbsp;
Ramonda got to meet her grandson before she died.

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:43.740
Then, the adorable kid tells his Aunt Shuri&nbsp;
that Toussaint is his Haitian name, but his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:49.680
real name is T'Challa. This new and unexpected&nbsp;
mid-credits information is an elegant solution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:54.360
to the problem of whether or not to cast a new&nbsp;
King T'Challa. Introducing the character's son&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.360 --> 00:10:00.000
allows Boseman to remain the sole actor to&nbsp;
portray King T'Challa, son of T'Chaka, while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:05.220
the next generation's T'Challa can eventually&nbsp;
become a part of the MCU is his own right.

